messages.content:
The test
['org.apache.commons.lang3.time.FastDateFormat_ParserTest.testLANG_832()', 'org.apache.commons.lang3.time.FastDateParserTest.testLANG_832()']
failed.

The test looks like: 
    @Test
    public void testLANG_832() throws Exception {
        testSdfAndFdp("'d'd" ,"d3", false); // OK
        testSdfAndFdp("'d'd'","d3", true); // should fail (unterminated quote)
    }

    @Test
    public void testLANG_832() throws Exception {
        testSdfAndFdp("'d'd" ,"d3", false); // OK
        testSdfAndFdp("'d'd'","d3", true); // should fail (unterminated quote)
    }

Current bug report: 
{
    "summary": "FastDateParser does not handle unterminated quotes correctly"
    "description": "FDP does not handled unterminated quotes the same way as SimpleDateFormat\nFor example:\nFormat: 'd'd'  \nDate: d3\nThis should fail to parse the format and date but it actually works.  \nThe format is parsed as:\nPattern: d(\\p\n{IsNd}\n++)\n"
}

Recent bug reports: 
RPT#LANG-831: {
 "summary": "FastDateParser does not handle white-space properly"
 "description": "The SimpleDateFormat Javadoc does not treat white-space specially, however FastDateParser treats a single white-space as being any number of white-space characters. This means that FDP will parse dates that fail when parsed by SDP."
}

RPT#LANG-826: {
 "summary": "FastDateParser does not handle non-ASCII digits correctly"
 "description": "The FastDateParser class assumes that all numbers are ascii digits. However, that is not the case for some locales such as  \nja\\_JP\\_JP, hi\\_IN, th\\_TH, and th\\_TH\\_TH\nRather than using \"\\d\", the code needs to match using \"\\p\n{IsNd}\n\" which is the Unicode class for numeric digits."
}

RPT#LANG-828: {
 "summary": "FastDateParser does not handle non-Gregorian calendars properly"
 "description": "The following calendars are non-Gregorian [1]:\nja\\_JP\\_JP - Japanese Imperial)  \nth\\_TH (with any variant) - Thai Buddhist\nThis causes errors when testing round-trip conversions. The simplest solution is to fall back to SimpleDateFormat for these calendars. [1] <http://docs.oracle.com/javase/6/docs/technotes/guides/intl/calendar.doc.html>"
}
